Details / evidence (how it works)

Granularity: per chunk
The writer serializes rows into chunk-sized buffers (default chunk_group_row_limit / chunkRowCount — default 10000 rows). When a chunk is serialized (SerializeChunkData) the code attempts to CompressBuffer() the chunk's serialized value buffer. If compression succeeds, the compressed buffer replaces the serialized buffer for storage and the chunk's metadata records the compression type and decompressed size. See SerializeChunkData and the CompressBuffer call in columnar_writer.c.
What is compressed
The column value buffer for each chunk (valueBuffer) is the thing that is compressed. The existsBuffer (null bitmap) is serialized separately and is not compressed (SerializeBoolArray -> existsBuffer is written unchanged).
Where compressed bytes are stored
Compressed value buffers (and uncompressed ones, when compression didn't help) are written into the stripe's "data section" in the relation file when the stripe is flushed (FlushStripe writes all exists buffers then value buffers). Stripe metadata and per-chunk skip nodes record offsets/lengths and compression metadata so reads can find & decompress each chunk.
Decompression
On read, DeserializeChunkData gets the chunkBuffers for a column/chunk and calls DecompressBuffer(chunkBuffers->valueBuffer, chunkBuffers->valueCompressionType, chunkBuffers->decompressedValueSize) to obtain the uncompressed chunk contents; deserialization then proceeds per chunk.
Metadata stored per chunk
Per-chunk skip node stores valueCompressionType, valueCompressionLevel, decompressedValueSize, offsets and lengths. That proves compression metadata is tracked at chunk granularity.
Supported algorithms and settings
Supported compression types (build-time): none, pglz, lz4, zstd (controlled by columnar.compression and columnar.compression_level GUCs or alter_columnar_table_set). Default is zstd if compiled in. See columnar_compression.c and columnar.c for GUC defaults.
When compression applies
Compression is applied when chunks are serialized (i.e., on writes/flush). The README notes the compression option affects newly-inserted data only; existing stripes/chunks are not automatically recompressed by changing the option.
Chunk / stripe size defaults (relevant to compression frequency)
Defaults in code: chunk_group_row_limit (chunk rows) default 10000; stripe_row_limit default 150000. These determine how often chunks and stripes are created and thus how often compression happens.
Short summary answer to your phrasing

Is it per stripe? No — compression is per chunk inside a stripe (each column's chunk value buffer).
Is it per chunk group? The code groups chunks into chunk groups and records chunk group row counts, but compression is applied to each chunk's value buffer individually.
Is it per data file or per table? No — compressed data is stored in the table's per-stripe data file, but compression decisions/metadata are recorded per chunk (not a single table-level/whole-file compression).
Does it address stripe/striping? Stripes are containers of chunks and the file layout is stripe‑oriented; compression occurs at chunk granularity within those stripes.
