Short answer

Hydra columnar reads and decompresses only the columns and chunk groups it needs for the query — it does not blindly decompress the whole table. Work is done stripe-by-stripe and chunk-by-chunk: the reader loads only projected columns and only the chunks that cannot be skipped by the skip-list/min-max checks, and decompression is done per chunk when that chunk is deserialized.
Memory usage depends on: (a) how many columns are projected, (b) how many chunks (in the stripe(s)) are selected, (c) compressed sizes and decompressed sizes of those chunks, and (d) the planner’s aggregation memory (hash table / work_mem) and any caching settings.
Why (what the code does)

Column projection: the reader builds a projected-column mask from the query’s target list; only those columns are loaded for a stripe (see ProjectedColumnMask).
Chunk skipping: before reading chunk data, Hydra builds a SelectedChunkMask using per-chunk skip nodes (min/max) and query predicates. Any chunk whose min/max refute the WHERE conditions is skipped entirely — no read, no decompression for that chunk.
Loading buffers: for each projected column in a stripe, the code reads the per-chunk "exists" buffer and the per-chunk "value" buffer (the stored bytes). These are stored in ColumnBuffers for the stripe.
Decompression granularity: when a chunk is actually deserialized, the extension calls DecompressBuffer(...) for that chunk’s value buffer and then DeserializeDatumArray(...) to get column values. Decompression happens per chunk (not per whole stripe or table).
Caching: there is an optional page cache feature (columnar_enable_page_cache) that, if enabled, can keep decompressed chunk buffers in a cache so repeat reads don’t recompress; by default it’s disabled. Cache size and behavior are controlled by GUCs.
Implications for GROUP BY / aggregate queries

If the GROUP BY and aggregates only reference N columns, the engine will only load/decompress those N columns (for the chunks that must be scanned).
If there are useful predicates on columns with min/max statistics, many chunks may be skipped entirely and won’t be read/decompressed.
If there are no predicates (full scan) and the GROUP BY references many columns (or the query planner needs many columns), the reader will load the selected columns’ chunk buffers for all chunks in each stripe it scans; then it will decompress per chunk as it processes the stripe.
Aggregation memory (hash aggregates) is separate: even if column decompression is limited, a large GROUP BY (many distinct groups) can cause large in-memory hash tables which are governed by PostgreSQL memory behavior (work_mem, spilling to disk for sorts in some plans). Parallel workers will multiply memory consumption per worker.
Practical details about memory footprint (important)

Compressed buffers are read from disk into memory (rawValueBuffer->len = compressed length). DecompressBuffer then allocates a decompressed buffer sized to the recorded decompressed size. During decompression you therefore may briefly have both compressed and decompressed buffers in memory. After decompression the decompressed buffer is kept in chunkData->valueBufferArray for that chunk until it’s freed at next chunk read; the original compressed buffer is kept with the ColumnChunkBuffers for the stripe metadata until the stripe is released.
Because LoadColumnBuffers reads compressed value buffers for all selected chunks of a projected column up front, the in-memory compressed footprint can be: sum(compressed sizes of selected chunks × projected columns) for the stripe(s) being processed.
The decompressed footprint is roughly: for the chunk(s) currently being deserialized, sum(decompressed sizes for those columns). Peak memory can be compressed + decompressed buffers together.
Limits/checks in code:
Chunk/value serialization enforces per-chunk size checks (e.g. ~1 GB guards and a per-datum 256 MB limit), but these are high; with many columns or many chunks you can easily reach large total memory usage.
Defaults: chunk_group_row_limit ≈ 10k rows, stripe_row_limit ≈ 150k rows. Bigger chunks/stripes → fewer I/O ops but larger per-chunk memory.
Recommendations to avoid OOM on large GROUP BYs

Project only the columns needed by the GROUP BY and aggregates.
Add selective WHERE predicates (on columns that have min/max stored) so SelectedChunkMask can skip chunks.
Reduce chunk / stripe sizes (columnar.chunk_group_row_limit, columnar.stripe_row_limit) to limit per-chunk/decompressed size (trade-off: more I/O overhead).
Use compression that yields smaller compressed sizes (zstd) to lower I/O and compressed-memory footprint (but decompressed size is unchanged).
Be careful with columnar_enable_page_cache: caching decompressed buffers reduces repeated decompression but increases resident memory use.
Tune PostgreSQL memory settings (work_mem, max_parallel_workers_per_gather) to control aggregate/hash memory and parallel-worker multiplication.
If possible, run heavy aggregations on fewer columns or push partial aggregation down / use GROUP BY on pre-aggregated data / increase node RAM.
