There is no one-size-fits-all: prefer many smaller columnar leaf partitions when most queries can be pruned to a small subset of partitions (so each query touches few tables/stripes/chunks). Prefer fewer larger columnar tables when queries routinely must scan most of the data (many partitions would only add planning and metadata overhead without saving memory).
In practice, if you can partition by a predicate users almost always filter on (time range, customer_id, etc.), make partitions small enough that a typical query touches only a few leaf tables — that will minimize reads/decompression and reduce memory pressure. If queries are global scans across time/customers, consolidate into fewer larger columnar tables and instead reduce stripe/chunk sizes and other memory settings.
Why — how hydra’s reader/writer behavior drives this tradeoff

Decompression and I/O are done per chunk (per column chunk inside a stripe). The system:
Loads only projected columns (ProjectedColumnMask).
Uses per-chunk min/max skip nodes to skip chunks when predicates allow (SelectedChunkMask).
Reads the compressed value buffer for every selected chunk of each projected column, then calls DecompressBuffer(chunk) and deserializes that chunk.
Memory usage per query therefore depends on:
how many partitions/leaf tables the planner selects,
how many stripes and chunks in those partitions are selected,
how many columns are projected, and
compressed and decompressed sizes of those chunks.
If partitioning lets the planner prune most partitions, the query will only read/decompress a small set of chunks → low memory. If pruning fails and the query scans many partitions, the memory footprint grows accordingly.
Concrete trade-offs

Many small partitions (pros)
Strong partition pruning reduces reads and decompression for targeted queries.
Smaller partitions → fewer rows per stripe/chunk (if stripes/chunks are similar), so smaller per-chunk decompressed memory.
Many small partitions (cons)
Planner overhead and longer planning times if queries touch many partitions.
More catalog metadata, more files/stripes to manage, more I/O metadata scanning.
If a query must touch many partitions, you may have to open/read many small files/chunks which can increase peak memory and I/O overhead.
Few large partitions (pros)
Lower catalog/planning overhead and fewer files to manage.
Better streaming/sequential I/O when you must scan everything.
Few large partitions (cons)
If queries typically target small ranges, you can’t prune and will read/decompress more data than necessary.
Practical knobs and heuristics to control memory

Use partitioning that matches query filters

Partition by the column most commonly used in WHERE/GROUP BY (time, customer_id).
Ensure planners can do partition pruning (use native Postgres partitioning, avoid constructs that defeat pruning).
Tune chunk/stripe sizes so per-chunk decompressed memory is reasonable

Defaults: chunk_group_row_limit ≈ 10,000 rows; stripe_row_limit ≈ 150,000 rows.
The code already prevents single-value buffers > ~1GB and uses ~1GB thresholds internally; that’s high — aim much lower.
Rule of thumb: choose chunk/stripe sizes so the decompressed size you actually need to hold in memory for a chunk (per projected column) is comfortably below available RAM. Example: if avg row size per column is 100 B and chunk rows = 10k → ~1 MB per column per chunk; multiply by number of projected columns to estimate peak decompressed chunk memory.
If your GROUP BY touches many columns, reduce chunk_row_count (columnar.chunk_group_row_limit) and/or stripe_row_limit to limit per-chunk decompressed buffers.
Compression and cache

Use zstd if available (smaller compressed read footprint reduces memory used for compressed buffers); set compression_level appropriately.
columnar_enable_page_cache can cache decompressed chunk buffers — this saves repeated decompression but increases resident memory. Leave it off if you’re memory-limited.
PostgreSQL planner / executor memory

Aggregation memory (hash tables) is separate from compression memory. Tune work_mem and parallelism:
Reduce max_parallel_workers_per_gather or columnar_min_parallel_processes so you don’t multiply memory usage across many parallel workers.
Ensure work_mem is sized so hash aggregates don’t consume excessive memory (or they’ll spill to disk but that costs I/O).
Monitor stripe / chunk stats and inspect storage

Use columnar.stats(rel) and columnar_storage_info() UDFs to see stripe sizes, chunk counts and per-stripe dataLength.
Query columnar.options and GUCs: SELECT * FROM columnar.options; show columnar.compression, columnar.stripe_row_limit, etc.
Balance partition count limits

Thousands of partitions is feasible, but planning and catalog bloat are real: if queries frequently scan many partitions, that kills planning time and increases metadata memory. If you already have ~1k partitions (you said ~1053 tables earlier), ensure typical queries prune to a small subset; otherwise consider fewer partitions or subpartitioning strategies.
Concrete suggested approach (step-by-step)

Measure: run columnar.stats() on sample partitions to get typical stripe/chunk compressed and decompressed sizes and rows per stripe.
Calculate typical per-query projected columns and average row size per projected column; estimate compressed + decompressed per-chunk memory.
If a typical query targets only a small time range / customer:
Use smaller partitions (e.g., monthly/daily) so pruning limits touched partitions to a few.
Keep default chunk/stripe settings or reduce chunk_group_row_limit to limit per-chunk decompression.
If typical queries scan broad ranges:
Consolidate to fewer partitions (quarterly/yearly), but reduce stripe_row_limit and chunk_group_row_limit so chunks are smaller and per-chunk memory is lower.
Tune compression: use zstd and increase compression_level to reduce compressed read footprint (but decomposition still needs decompressed RAM).
Limit parallel workers for heavy queries and tune work_mem.
Example numbers (for sizing only)

Suppose avg row across the projected columns is 200 bytes, chunk_row_count = 10,000 → decompressed ≈ 2 MB per column per chunk. If your query projects 200 columns, decompressed = 400 MB per chunk; if multiple chunks are processed concurrently (or multiple partitions), this multiplies. So reducing columns, chunk rows, or stripe rows will reduce peak memory.
Aim to keep peak decompressed memory per process well below available RAM (and remember multiple worker processes will multiply usage).
